  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 AM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026 \n\nToday-Tonight...Pressure gradient remains relatively tight across \nthe area between high pressure to the north and a weak frontal \nboundary to the south. This will continue a breezy onshore flow \ntoday as northeast winds increase from 10-15 mph this morning up to \n15-20 mph this afternoon, with gusts around 25-30 mph. Some drier \nair builds in, lowering rain chances today. However, the breezy \nwinds and sufficient low level moisture will still lead to isolated \nto scattered showers that push onshore and inland through the day. \nPoPs for much of the area will reside around 20-40 percent, with \ngreatest coverage of showers and potential for a few storms mainly \nsoutheast of the I-4 corridor. Any storms and heavier showers will \nhave the potential to produce gusty winds up to 35-40 mph as well as \nlocally heavy downpours of 1-3 inches. As a mid/upper level low \napproaches from the east and onshore flow continues, isolated to \nscattered onshore moving showers and storms will continue into \ntonight, with greatest chance for rainfall along the coast. \n\nDrier air may make it feel a little less humid, mainly near to NW of \nI-4 where dewpoints are forecast to fall into the upper 60s this \nafternoon. Highs will be near to just a tad below normal, in the mid \nto upper 80s, with lows tonight in the 70s. \n\nAt the beaches, a high risk of life threatening rip currents will \npersist today, along with rough surf. Heed the advice of lifeguards, \nbeach patrol flags, and signs. Entering the surf is not advised!  \n\nFriday-Sunday...Surface high pressure to the north builds southward, \nwith pressure gradient weakening. This will allow low level winds to \ncontinue and also decrease through late week and into the weekend. \nStrong ridge to the northwest of Florida lingers through the period, \nas a mid/upper level low moves in from the east across the state and \nslowly progresses westward into the weekend. Moisture will increase \nwith PW values rising to 1.7\" Friday and 1.9-2.0\" into the weekend. \nPassing low aloft will aid in lift and also lead to cooler temps at \n500mb (as low as -8 to -10C Friday into Saturday). This will lead to \nincreasing shower and storm chances, with scattered to numerous \nshowers and storms developing each day (rain chances near to above \nnormal). A few stronger storms will be possible each day due to \nlingering dry air aloft, and the cool temps in the mid levels. Main \nstorm threats will be frequent lightning strikes, gusty winds to 40-\n50 mph and locally heavy rainfall of 1-3\", which may result in minor \nflooding issues. Onshore flow will continue low end rain chances \nalong the coast each night as isolated to scattered showers and \nstorms across the waters may reach the coast. Temperatures remain \nseasonable, with highs in the upper 80s and lows in the 70s. \n\nMonday-Wednesday...Low aloft shifts west, with a weak sfc trough \nnear to north of the area. Winds remain relatively weak and mostly \nonshore through early to middle portion of next week. Sufficient \nmoisture remains in place to continue scattered to numerous showers \nand storms each afternoon and evening as sea breeze shifts inland. \nIsolated strong storms will continue to be possible each day, with \ntemperatures remaining close to normal values for this time of\nyear.",
